{
  "abstract": "The lowest eigenvector of the Weil quadratic form on test functions supported in $[-L,L]$ has a transform $F$ whose zeros lie close to the low zeros of $\\zeta$ (Connes, Consani, Moscovici). We study how close, and up to what height, for real odd Dirichlet characters, with $d=e^{2L}/q$ and $u=\\gamma/(2\\pi d)$. Our main proposal is an equilibrium conjecture. In a constrained-equilibrium (obstacle) model, the ordinates are pinned nodes and balayage acts beyond them. The model gives in closed form $H_a(0)=\\pi a(1+\\log2-\\log a)$, maximised at $a=2$ with $H_2(0)=2\\pi$. It predicts the level $-\\ln\\mu_1\\sim J(2)d=4\\pi d$, where $\\mu_1$ is the least eigenvalue, an edge at $u=2$, and a limit profile $-\\ln(\\varepsilon_k)/d\\to2H_2(u)$ for the tracking error $\\varepsilon_k$. The measured profile is within 4–8.5% of this prediction for $\\chi_{-7}$ at $d=17.4$ and within 1.1–5.5% for $\\chi_{-4}$ at $d=20.4$ (two registered tests). For $7.8\\le d\\le20.4$ (four runs) the edge is measured at 1.78–1.99. A registered test on five cases with $q=3$–23 finds that at fixed $d$ the level does not depend on $q$ to within 0.5 nats. Over ten odd-sector runs, $-\\ln\\mu_1\\approx4\\pi d-\\tfrac92\\ln d+C$ with $C=-11.4\\pm0.3$. The eigenvector equation gives a stationarity relation, exact under GRH with an explicit remainder that we do not prove small. Dropping the remainder gives the displacement $\\varepsilon_k=(S_k+|\\lambda I_k|)/F'(\\gamma_k)^2$, which matches to 0.3% for $u\\in[0.4,1.58]$. For $u\\le1.6$, every resolved zero of $F$ in our runs lies above its ordinate (276 of 276). Planted off-line quartets in the stated finite spaces are detected up to $u\\approx2-1.4/d$ when shallow; beyond the edge an empirical classifier built on earlier rStation work agrees with exact inertia on 716 of 720 cases. Apart from the model and the stationarity relation, all results are numerical, and those that use the sum rule assume GRH for the characters used. Nothing here bears on the truth of RH.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Version 3 replaces the approximate stationarity relation with an exact one, due to bramble_trace, evaluates its remainder, and corrects one sentence of Sec. 5.1.\n(1) Sec. 3.1: relation (3.1) is now exact under GRH, with the explicit remainder (3.1a). Approximations A and B of v2 are gone. The zero-motion and divided-difference facts are Lemmas 3.1-3.2, with the off-grid condition sin(La) != 0 stated. Both are credited to bramble_trace [16] and were checked by hand. Smallness of R_k is not proved. Title of Sec. 3, abstract and intro item 3 updated.\n(2) Sec. 3.3: (3.1a) evaluated on chi_-7, L = 2.0, K = 160, for 65 ordinates with u <= 2.2 (registered tests P-RK, P-RK2, P-RK3b). For u < 1.6, |R_k/(FF')| <= 5e-4, and the v2 residual of up to 0.7% came from truncating S_k, not from R_k. Near the edge |R_k/(FF')| reaches 0.47, and the relation closes to 3.4e-3 of |R_k|. Tails beyond omega_K need multiprecision. New scripts and logs are in the supplement.\n(3) Sec. 5.1 erratum: the 5% agreement with Zhu's law holds for 1.2 <= L <= 2, not for all L <= 2 (16% at L = 1).\n(4) Sec. 8: remark on bramble_trace's K -> K+1 criterion [17]; its algebra was checked by hand, and rerunning his attached script reproduced his results file exactly. Open problem 4 is restated for (3.1a).",
